  abstract = {As an emerging swarm intelligence algorithm that simulates the intelligent behavior of greylag geese groups, the Greylag Goose Optimization Algorithm (GGO) demonstrates excellent global optimal capability capabilities by mimicking the exploration behavior of geese during foraging and the exploitation strategy when avoiding predators. However, it suffers from inherent limitations, including slow convergence, diminishing population diversity, and inadequate local search precision. To address these issues, this paper proposes an improved Greylag Goose Optimization Algorithm (IGGO). At the core of the first improvement is an adaptive convergence factor, By modifying the convergence factor a thereby orchestrates a dynamic transition between the exploration and exploitation behaviors throughout the search process. Secondly, a controllable perturbation mechanism (CDM) is designed to enhance population diversity by applying directional perturbations in the neighbourhood of individuals, perform perturbation on each solution during each iteration process, thereby avoiding premature convergence. To validate the improvements, we benchmarked IGGO against standard GGO and three other mainstream swarm intelligence algorithms using the CEC 2022 test suite. Their performance was evaluated based on four metrics: average value, standard deviation, execution time, and best value. Experimental results demonstrate that IGGO exhibits superior comprehensive performance on most benchmark functions, p},
}

